  "examples": [
   {
    "title": "A non-inverter fridge on its own",
    "text": "Running 200 W, starting 1,200 W. The start is larger than 200 W × 1.25, so it sets the size: 1,200 W ÷ 0.8 ÷ 1,000 = 1.5 kVA, the smallest common size of 2.5 kVA."
   },
   {
    "title": "A 1.5 ton non-inverter AC on its own",
    "text": "Running 1,800 W, starting 5,400 W. The compressor start needs 6.75 kVA, so a 7.5 kVA generator just for the AC."
   },
   {
    "title": "Fridge, pump and a 1.5 ton non-inverter AC with fans and lights",
    "text": "Running 3,440 W. The fans, the fridge, the pump and the AC all have motors, but only the biggest jump, the AC compressor's, is added: peak 7,040 W, 8.8 kVA, so 10 kVA."
   },
   {
    "title": "The same house with a 1.5 ton inverter AC",
    "text": "Running 3,090 W. The inverter AC has almost no jump, so the pump's start is now the largest: peak 4,890 W, 6.11 kVA, so 6.5 kVA instead of 10 kVA."
   }
  ],
