     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the starting watts of an AC?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "For non-inverter split ACs about 3,600 W (1 ton), 5,400 W (1.5 ton) and 7,200 W (2 ton). Inverter ACs have no hard start; their highest draw is the maximum input, about 1,900 W for 1.5 ton."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I calculate surge watts for a generator?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Add the running watts of everything that will be on, then add the largest single jump (starting watts minus running watts) of any one motor or compressor. That total is the surge the generator must supply."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I add the starting watts of all my appliances?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "No. Add all running watts, then only the biggest starting jump. In the default list the pump, the fridge and the washer all have a jump, but only the pump's is added, giving 3,840 W."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long do starting watts last?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Usually a second or two for a fridge, pump or AC compressor, a little longer for a heavily loaded pump. Short as it is, the generator must deliver it or the voltage collapses and the motor stalls."
      }
